tls: mbedtls: client: never offer the h3 family over TCP

Since upstream c817c5292 ("tls: mbedtls: C-675: a client offers only the
ALPN it asked for") a TLS-over-TCP client offers exactly its connect info's
ALPN list (stash CIS_ALPN) and no longer falls back to the vhost's alpn.
Since upstream 4761cafff ("client: an alpn list without h3 pins the
protocol against alt-svc"), a connect info ALPN list that leaves h3 out is
taken as the user pinning the protocol: neither a learned Alt-Svc nor a
remembered h3 negotiation races QUIC for it any more.

Together they leave an HTTP client that wants both h2 over TCP and h3
upgrades no list to give: "h2,http/1.1" never upgrades to h3 (txiki.js:
tests/test-fetch-h3-post.js and test-fetch-h3-native-cold-upgrade.js), and
"h2,http/1.1,h3" offers h3 on the TCP handshake, where some servers
(Fastly) select it and the connection is left with no role that can carry
it (txiki.js: tests/test-fetch-alpn-no-h3-over-tcp.js).

Mirror the QUIC case just above (which keeps the TCP ALPNs off a QUIC
handshake): on a non-QUIC connection, drop "h3" and "h3-*" entries from
the list before handing it to mbedtls.  The list on the connection still
names h3, so connect2's alt-svc / h3 race logic sees the user's wish; only
the TCP wire offer changes.  If nothing is left, no ALPN is offered.

Not upstream: v5.1.0 (5f39aa483) and main (d954e68f2) offer the stash list
verbatim.  Upstreamable (the openssl / other backends would want the same).

---
diff --git a/lib/tls/mbedtls/mbedtls-client.c b/lib/tls/mbedtls/mbedtls-client.c
index 9021df9ad..d4350ef18 100644
--- a/lib/tls/mbedtls/mbedtls-client.c
+++ b/lib/tls/mbedtls/mbedtls-client.c
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@ lws_ssl_client_bio_create(struct lws *wsi)
 {
 	struct lws_tls_conn *conn;
 	char hostname[LWS_TLS_CLIENT_HOSTNAME_LEN];
-	char temp_alpn[128];
+	char temp_alpn[128], tcp_alpn[128];
 	const char *alpn_comma = NULL;
 
 	if (wsi->stash)
@@ -163,6 +163,42 @@ lws_ssl_client_bio_create(struct lws *wsi)
 			alpn_comma = temp_alpn;
 	}
 
+	if (alpn_comma && !(wsi->role_ops &&
+			    !strcmp(wsi->role_ops->name, "quic")) &&
+	    strstr(alpn_comma, "h3")) {
+		/*
+		 * The mirror of the QUIC case: TLS over TCP cannot carry the
+		 * h3 family, and a server that selects h3 from a TCP offer
+		 * (eg, Fastly) leaves the connection with no role that can
+		 * use it.  The connection's list still names h3 when its user
+		 * wants alt-svc / QUIC upgrades (connect2 takes a list without
+		 * h3 as the user pinning the protocol), so keep h3 off the
+		 * wire here rather than out of the list.
+		 */
+		const char *p = alpn_comma;
+		char *o = tcp_alpn, *oe = tcp_alpn + sizeof(tcp_alpn) - 1;
+
+		while (*p) {
+			const char *e = strchr(p, ',');
+			size_t l = e ? lws_ptr_diff_size_t(e, p) : strlen(p);
+
+			if (l && !(l >= 2 && p[0] == 'h' && p[1] == '3' &&
+				   (l == 2 || p[2] == '-'))) {
+				if (o != tcp_alpn && o < oe)
+					*o++ = ',';
+				if (l > lws_ptr_diff_size_t(oe, o))
+					break;
+				memcpy(o, p, l);
+				o += l;
+			}
+			p += l;
+			if (*p == ',')
+				p++;
+		}
+		*o = '\0';
+		alpn_comma = tcp_alpn[0] ? tcp_alpn : NULL;
+	}
+
 	if (alpn_comma) {
 		lwsl_info("%s: %s: client conn sending ALPN list '%s'\n",
 			  __func__, lws_wsi_tag(wsi), alpn_comma);
